# governed-roots.txt.example - OPTIONAL. Copy to governed-roots.txt (same folder) to use it.
#
# A "governed" folder is one Mnemosyne must never index by default and must never write its own
# database, logs or watch list into - for example a folder that holds a live production system.
# With no governed-roots.txt present, nothing is governed; that is the normal state for most installs.
#
# Format: one ABSOLUTE folder path per line. Lines starting with '#' and blank lines are ignored.
#   - A bare drive root (C:\ or D:/) is IGNORED and reported by `status` and `ingest` - it would mean
#     "the whole drive", which is never what a governed line is for.
#   - A drive-relative or relative path (C:, C:folder, folder\sub) is IGNORED and reported too.
#   - A folder is matched by spelling AND by file identity, so a junction, symlink or alternate
#     spelling (\\?\C:\..., \\localhost\C$\...) that reaches it is governed as well.
#
# To index a governed folder anyway, pass `ingest <path> --allow-governed` deliberately.
# Writing Mnemosyne's own files into a governed folder is refused with no override.
#
# Windows paths only in this release: the parser accepts a drive path (C:\...) or a UNC path
# (\\server\share\...). A POSIX path such as /srv/x is IGNORED and reported, so on Mac/Linux this file
# currently protects nothing.
#
# Example (replace with your own path):
# C:\path\to\production-system
